.he "awk (1)                     9/22/26                      awk (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
awk
- pattern scanning and processing language
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
awk [ -Fc ] [ -f progfile | 'program' ] [ var=value ... ] [ file ... ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Awk scans each input file for records that match any of a set of patterns
given in the program, which appears literally on the command line or in a
file named by -f progfile.  With each pattern there can be an action, run
when a record matches.  A pattern with no action prints the record; an
action with no pattern runs for every record.  BEGIN and END match before
the first record and after the last.
.sp
Files are read in order; with no files, the standard input is read.  A
file name of - means the standard input, and an argument of the form
var=value is an assignment rather than a file name.
.sp
awk is 2.11BSD awk, ported to micronix.  There is no floating point:
numbers are 32-bit signed integers, so awk's numeric model and the
transcendental builtins differ from a full awk.  See NUMBERS and
LIMITATIONS below.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-Fc	Set the input field separator to the single character c, as if by FS=c.  -Ft sets it to a tab.

.ti -10
-f progfile	Take the program from progfile.

.ti -10
'program'	The program itself, as the first argument.

.ti -10
var=value	Assign var before scanning.  The assignment happens after BEGIN, which therefore cannot see it.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
RECORDS AND FIELDS
.in +5
.br
The input is a sequence of records separated by RS, a newline by
default.  Each record is split into fields on the field separator FS,
which is a single blank by default; a blank FS means runs of blanks and
tabs separate fields, and leading blanks are stripped.
.sp
$0 is the whole record, $1 through $NF its fields, and $(expr) refers to
a field indirectly.  NF is the number of fields in the current record,
NR the number of records read so far, and FILENAME the name of the
current input file.  Setting FS or RS changes the way later records are
split; setting NF truncates or extends the fields.
.sp
OFS is written between fields by print, ORS at the end of a record; both
default as for input.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
PATTERNS
.in +5
.br
A pattern selects the records whose action runs.  The forms are:
.sp
.nf
	/regex/		$0 matches the regular expression
	expr relop expr	the six comparisons, and ~ !~ for match
	pattern && pattern   logical and
	pattern || pattern   logical or
	! pattern		logical not
	pattern, pattern	every record from the first that matches
				the first pattern through the next that
				matches the second
	BEGIN		before the first record
	END		after the last
.fi
.sp
An expression alone is true when it is both non-empty and non-zero; a
missing pattern matches every record.  A comparison is not a general
expression and can appear only here and in if, while and for, not in an
assignment or an argument to print.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
STATEMENTS
.in +5
.br
An action is a sequence of statements separated by semicolons, newlines
or right braces.  The statements are:
.sp
.in +10
.ti -10
if ( cond ) statement [ else statement ]	Run the statement if cond is true, the else one otherwise.

.ti -10
while ( cond ) statement	Repeat while cond is true.

.ti -10
for ( init ; cond ; incr ) statement	Run init once, then repeat statement while cond is true, running incr after each.

.ti -10
for ( var in array ) statement	Run statement with var set to each subscript of array in turn.

.ti -10
break	Leave the innermost enclosing while or for.

.ti -10
continue	Begin the next iteration of the innermost enclosing while or for.

.ti -10
next	Read the next record and start the pattern-action sequence over.

.ti -10
exit [ expr ]	Stop, with expr as the status if given.

.ti -10
print [ list ] [ > file | >> file | | cmd ]	Print $0, or list, to the standard output or the redirected file.

.ti -10
printf format [ , list ] [ > file | ... ]	Print list formatted as format, as in the C library.

.ti -10
getline	Read the next record into $0 and set NF, NR and FILENAME; 1 if a record was read, 0 at end of input.

.ti -10
{ list }	A group; a statement like any other.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXPRESSIONS
.in +5
.br
The operators of an expression, highest precedence first:
.sp
.nf
	$		field, and $expr for the indirect field
	++ --		increment and decrement, before or after
	- +		unary minus and plus
	* / %		multiply, divide, remainder
	+ -		add, subtract
	(space)		string concatenation
	= += -= *= /= %=	assignment
.fi
.sp
Concatenation is written as juxtaposition, so x y is x followed by y;
parentheses are needed in x (y) z to keep x from joining y.  A string
that looks like a number is a number in an arithmetic context, and a
number is a string in a string context.
.sp
The comparisons < <= > >= == !=, the match operators ~ !~, and the
logical !, && and || are not expression operators: they combine
conditions, so they appear only in a pattern or in if, while and for.
A comparison is numeric when both sides are numeric, otherwise the
strings are compared.  There is no ternary operator.
.sp
Array subscripts are strings, so arrays are associative.  There is no
declaration: an array grows as subscripts are assigned, and for (var in
array) visits them in no particular order.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
BUILTIN VARIABLES
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
NR	records read so far.

.ti -10
NF	fields in the current record.

.ti -10
FILENAME	the current input file.

.ti -10
FS	the input field separator, blank by default.

.ti -10
OFS	the output field separator, blank by default.

.ti -10
ORS	the output record separator, newline by default.

.ti -10
RS	the input record separator, newline by default.

.ti -10
OFMT	the format for printing numbers, %.6g by default; see NUMBERS.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
BUILTIN FUNCTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
length [ s ]	The length of s, or of $0 with no argument.

.ti -10
substr ( s , m [ , n ] )	The substring of s starting at character m, at most n characters long; to the end with no n.

.ti -10
index ( s , t )	The position in s where t first occurs, 1 for the start, or 0 if t is not in s.

.ti -10
split ( s , a [ , fs ] )	Split s into a[1], a[2], ... on the separator fs (FS by default), returning the number of fields.

.ti -10
sprintf ( format , ... )	Format as printf, returning the result as a string.

.ti -10
int ( x )	The integer part of x.

.ti -10
log ( x ) , exp ( x ) , sqrt ( x )	Integer approximations of the natural logarithm, its inverse, and the square root.  See NUMBERS.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NUMBERS
.in +5
.br
All numbers are 32-bit signed integers, from -2147483648 to
2147483647.  There is no floating point, and no soft-float library:
arithmetic is exact, division truncates toward zero, and % is the
remainder.  A string in a numeric context takes the leading integer of
what it spells, so "3.14" + 0 is 3 and "-7" is -7; a string with no
leading digits is zero.
.sp
The transcendental builtins collapse to integer approximations: log(x)
is roughly the natural logarithm (scaled floor(log2 x)), exp(x) is a
power of two close to e^x, and sqrt(x) is the integer square root.  A
number always prints as a plain decimal integer - OFMT is kept for
compatibility but has no effect, and %f, %e and %g in printf all print
the integer.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
REGULAR EXPRESSIONS
.in +5
.br
Regular expressions are those of egrep.  The operators are:
.sp
.nf
	.	any character
	[...]	one of the enclosed characters; a-z is a range
	[^...]	one character not enclosed
	^ $	start and end of the string
	* + ?	zero or more, one or more, zero or one
	|	alternation
	( )	grouping
.fi
.sp
Inside [...] a backslash quotes a character, so \] is a literal bracket,
and \n and \t are newline and tab.  Elsewhere \ddd is the character in
octal and \c the literal character c.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
PRINTF
.in +5
.br
printf and sprintf take a format of the C library's form.  The
conversions are %d (a 32-bit decimal, always), %o and %x (octal and
hexadecimal of the low 16 bits), %lo and %lx (of the whole 32 bits),
%c (a character), %s (a string), and %f, %e and %g (the integer, its
precision ignored).  Width, and the - and 0 flags, are honoured.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
LIMITATIONS
.in +5
.br
Deviations from a full awk:
.sp
.in +10
.ti -10
No floating point.  Numbers are integers, as under NUMBERS.

.ti -10
A small builtin set.  There are no sub, gsub, match, close or system, no
delete for arrays, no do/while, and no user-defined functions.

.ti -10
getline is bare only.  There is no getline var and no getline < file,
and neither print | cmd nor cmd | getline works, because micronix has no
shell to pipe through.

.ti -10
A comparison is not a general expression.  It can appear only in a
pattern or in if, while and for; x = (a == b) and print (a == b) are
syntax errors, and print a > b is always redirection, never a
comparison.

.ti -10
NF is 0 in an END block.  The empty record read at end of file is
re-split.

.ti -10
No -v flag.  A var=value argument is applied after BEGIN runs.

.ti -10
The port's own knobs are not standard: -d and -l turn on debugging, -S
saves the compiled program to awk.out and -R thaws one back.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLES
.in +5
.br
Print lines longer than 72 characters:
.nf
	length > 72
.fi
.sp
Print the first two fields in opposite order:
.nf
	{ print $2, $1 }
.fi
.sp
Sum the first column, print the sum and average:
.nf
	{ s += $1 }
	END { print "sum is", s, " average is", s/NR }
.fi
.sp
Count how often each word appears:
.nf
	{ for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) count[$i]++ }
	END { for (w in count) print count[w], w }
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
sed (1), grep (1), lex (1)
